#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e4a8430-6b20-5a38-879f-aed55131dec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/5e316ceff92e78155ee352a2e5c3c526d30852fb1803fd9cfb938bea3e5cf984.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339282} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0afb26-d708-5013-83f4-7d011a687b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/dfa05a8c2c943d5f74e5e5fef468006117c97b2d3d0a7b7a6b9c2024b6089272.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339283} {:answer/id #uuid "00397b04-978a-5183-bc58-165cbe505603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/d3f815e4205f0a24a125162072843fe507d0683ce40a89fdd13ee29c296f7500.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339284} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa6565e-339f-5618-9b98-de9e108cd1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/b6fe02d5b3d96aeaa6a4edc9a027ec69993330252322e10a57618dd499bb02b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339285} {:answer/id #uuid "d91a02e3-6aaa-5ee7-864a-e5b3a2781b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/6ba878568654dc85dba3ac561617132f851e62b63473eb8239fa716f53cd0dcd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa6565e-339f-5618-9b98-de9e108cd1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/b6fe02d5b3d96aeaa6a4edc9a027ec69993330252322e10a57618dd499bb02b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339285} :answer-field/id #uuid "156c3aba-8995-5b5c-b49c-308998a55d7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "873c9a2b-8c1b-5842-a449-297472752d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-147605" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/add078cb1b05f772f25c91a4736f5aacd4490ff8556ce6a0d0ea7ad44869d0e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,10)$, $(0,0),$ $(4,-20).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/8c86cb108725b3f1c7b5731dda0f11a5cb21a7daa023c5153cce1ece3ba6ba0f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb34d14-4e6b-5921-bbae-0804ad06b4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102206} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b33753-c1ca-54f2-b9c6-98ba27f6fa8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102207} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1ac350-98a0-5f6c-8490-7f08a97c5de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102208} {:answer/id #uuid "1338e2d6-476a-5cc4-a9f7-69b9fb7f0f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102209} {:answer/id #uuid "39e1c3d2-cf9c-5c0f-ac61-180359ad5ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1338e2d6-476a-5cc4-a9f7-69b9fb7f0f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102209} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d6bd43-1322-57b8-8f87-0e031cdaba61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba678a53-c013-5d44-b391-2d5cc0a9cc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-162150" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}]}
